<Project>

  <!--
  ============================================================================
  Directory.Build.props — repo-wide MSBuild defaults.
  ============================================================================

  This file is automatically picked up by MSBuild for every project under
  the repo root (csproj files inherit these properties unless they override
  them). It enforces a uniform, opinionated, enterprise-grade compilation
  policy across all TakOne projects (Domain, Application, Infrastructure,
  WebUI, Analyzers, SharedKernel).

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (v4 brutal-review finding #34):
    The repo had no Directory.Build.props anywhere. Each csproj declared its
    own (minimal) settings, and warnings accumulated silently — including
    nullable-warning suppressions (#pragma warning disable CS8618) scattered
    across 15 entity files. The Round 18 commit message could claim "0
    warnings, 0 errors" only because warnings were never errors. This file
    flips that posture.

  POLICY:
    * TreatWarningsAsErrors=true — a warning is a bug. Either fix the
      underlying issue or, if the warning is a false positive, suppress it
      with a scoped #pragma + a justifying comment. NO blanket suppressions.
    * Nullable=enable — the codebase already opted in per-project; this
      enforces it repo-wide for any future project added without explicit
      settings.
    * ImplicitUsings=enable — same rationale as Nullable.
    * AnalysisLevel=latest-recommended — opt in to the latest Roslyn
      analyzer rules (catches more bugs at compile time).
    * EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true — .editorconfig code-style rules run as
      build-time errors (not just IDE squiggles).
    * GenerateDocumentationFile is deliberately NOT set to true here.
      Enabling it would surface CS1591 (missing XML doc comment) on every
      public member — a cascade that would block the build until every
      public API is documented. The codebase has rich XML docs on most
      public surfaces already; enabling GenerateDocumentationFile is a
      separate, dedicated cleanup PR.

  PHASED WARNINGS-NOT-AS-ERRORS:
    The following analyzer codes are NOT treated as errors at this stage
    because they require broader refactoring that would be high-risk to
    do in a single PR without test coverage. They will be flipped to errors
    in a dedicated follow-up PR. This is the standard enterprise rollout
    pattern for TreatWarningsAsErrors on a codebase with accumulated
    technical debt: lock down NEW code (the warning-as-error gate) while
    deferring the existing-debt codes to per-code cleanup PRs where each
    site can be reviewed with focused attention.

    * CA1848 (Use LoggerMessage delegates) — 530 sites in Application alone.
      Requires adding [LoggerMessage] partial methods to every logging
      class. High-risk without integration tests to catch log-message-
      template drift.

    * CA1873 (Logger argument evaluation may be unnecessary) — 158 sites,
      same root cause as CA1848. Fixed automatically once the LoggerMessage
      source generator refactor lands.

    * CA1711 (Identifiers should not have certain suffixes) — 8 sites where
      Wolverine domain-event handler classes end in "EventHandler". This is
      the framework's own naming convention (Wolverine + MediatR both use
      "*Handler" / "*EventHandler" suffixes). Renaming would break the
      convention. Suppress at the analyzer level.

    * CA1707 (Identifiers should not contain underscores) — 272 sites in
      UnexpectedErrorCodes + Razor @inject field names. The codebase uses
      underscores as a deliberate naming convention: e.g.
      UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_RestockDialogOpen (the underscore
      separates the page/aggregate from the error category). This is
      project-wide and renaming would lose the readability benefit.

    * CS8602 (Dereference of a possibly null reference) — 84 sites, mostly
      in the Round 17 + Round 18 test-suite expansion where property-based
      tests dereference Result<T>.Value without an explicit .IsSuccess
      guard. Each site needs per-call analysis: some are real
      NullReferenceException risks (need an IsSuccess guard or a
      null-forgiving `!` with justification), others are false positives
      where the test pre-condition guarantees success. Rushing 84 sites in
      one PR risks subtle test-control-flow regressions. Deferred to a
      dedicated NRT-hardening PR with per-site review.

    * CA1826 (Do not use Enumerable methods on indexable collections) —
      24 sites. Performance hint, NOT a correctness bug. Replacing
      LINQ FirstOrDefault/LastOrDefault with an indexer requires verifying
      the collection type is indexable at each site (List<T>, T[],
      IReadOnlyList<T>) and that the empty-collection semantics are
      preserved. Deferred to a perf-cleanup PR alongside CA1861.

    * CA1861 (Prefer 'static readonly' fields over constant array
      arguments) — 18 sites. Performance hint. Mechanical per-site fix
      (extract the inline array literal to a private static readonly
      field) but requires per-site field-name choice. Deferred to the
      perf-cleanup PR alongside CA1826.

    * CA1859 (Use specific types for improved performance) — 2 sites in
      test code that hold a List<T> or AssemblyBuilder via a base
      interface (IReadOnlyList<T> or Assembly). Changing the declared
      type leaks implementation details into the public surface and
      requires design-level review. Deferred to a focused PR.

    * CA1825 (Avoid zero-length array allocations) — EF Core's
      `dotnet ef migrations add` scaffolder generates
      `defaultValue: new byte[0]` for every `rowversion` column (one
      per aggregate root — 8 sites in a fresh Initial scaffold: Users,
      SystemSettings, Sales, Products, Notifications, CustomerGroups,
      Categories, BroadcastNotifications). This is auto-generated code
      that regenerates on every scaffold; manually patching
      `new byte[0]` -> `Array.Empty<byte>()` is fragile (the change is
      lost on the next scaffold) and doesn't scale (the user's workflow
      is: delete migrations -> scaffold fresh -> update database). The
      modern .NET JIT recognizes `new byte[0]` and substitutes the
      cached `Array.Empty<byte>()` singleton at runtime, so there is
      no allocation overhead in practice. Deferred repo-wide because
      the only sites that fire are in scaffolded migration code.

    The codes that ARE errors today (CA1000, CA1852, CA1805, CA2211,
    CS8618, CA1844, CA1835, CA1510, CA1725, CA1305, CA2263, etc.) are
    fixed inline or with scoped [SuppressMessage] attributes with
    justifications.
  ============================================================================
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      Phased rollout: codes deferred to a dedicated cleanup PR.
      See the class-level comment above for the rationale per code.
      ORDER NOTE: codes are grouped by deferral reason (logging source-
      generator refactor / framework naming convention / deliberate
      project-wide convention / NRT hardening / perf cleanup / design
      review / migration scaffolding), not alphabetically. This keeps
      related codes adjacent so the cleanup-PR scope is obvious from
      the list.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>
      CA1848;CA1873;CA1711;CA1707;
      CS8602;
      CA1826;CA1861;
      CA1859;
      CA1825
    </WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
